  "profiling": {
    "status": "profiled",
    "method": "pandas profile of the committed data in this repository",
    "tables": 1
  },
  "summary": "Nine relational files from the UCI Restaurant & Consumer Data collection \u2014 user profiles, payment methods, cuisine preferences, ratings, and restaurant attributes \u2014 merged into a single analysis table for causal attribution of restaurant ratings.",
  "provenance": {
    "source": "UC Irvine Machine Learning Repository \u2014 Restaurant & Consumer Data",
    "link": "https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/232/restaurant+consumer+data",
    "snapshot": "2012 UCI release (data collected in Mexico)",
    "collection": "Collected via a recommender-system study of restaurant consumers in Mexico. Users self-reported profile attributes (including personality, interest, and color preference items) and rated restaurants they had visited. This is a convenience sample of study participants, not a representative population sample \u2014 a limitation that carries directly into every effect estimate.",
    "license_terms": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) per UCI ML Repository terms. Citation of the original donors is required.",
    "maintenance": "Static UCI archival dataset; no updates since deposit. final_data.pkl is regenerated by re-running get_initial_data() in 01_Data_Analysis/data_pipeline.py."
  },
  "labeling": "The outcome `rating` and the mediators `food_rating` and `service_rating` are self-reported ordinal scores given by users, not expert annotations. Self-report introduces common-method bias: the same person supplies both treatment and outcome measures in a single sitting.",
  "preprocessing": "Nine CSVs merged with pandas left joins. The six one-to-many attribute tables (userpayment, usercuisine, chefmozaccepts, chefmozparking, chefmozcuisine) are first collapsed to one row per key with their values joined into ';'-delimited sets, and chefmozhours4 is reduced to its weekday row; rating_final is then the only intentional fan-out. User chain: userprofile -> userpayment -> usercuisine -> rating_final; restaurant chain: geoplaces2 -> chefmozaccepts -> chefmozparking -> chefmozcuisine -> chefmozhours4; combined on placeID. Result: 1,161 rows x 52 features - one row per (userID, placeID) rating, enforced by a row-count guard in merge_data(). Engineered: geodesic patron-restaurant distance (Haversine), KMeans location clusters (k=5), age groups from birth year, Jaccard cuisine-match score, and business-hours categories. '?' markers replaced with NaN, then most-frequent imputation across 17 categorical columns, then label encoding.",
  "splits": "No train/test split \u2014 this is estimation, not prediction. Identification comes from DoWhy backdoor adjustment on the assumed graph, with propensity-score / IPW weighting for the heterogeneous treatment effect analysis.",
  "sensitive_data": "Person-level attributes are used directly as treatment variables: whether the user has children (hijos), height, personality type, interest category, and color preference. Birth year is present and binned into age groups. These are individual characteristics of study participants, and several have no plausible causal mechanism on restaurant ratings \u2014 treating them as levers would be both statistically and ethically unsound.",
  "recommended_uses": [
    "Teaching causal graph construction, backdoor identification, mediation, and HTE.",
    "Illustrating how graph-informed estimates diverge from naive correlations."
  ],
  "discouraged_uses": [
    "Any inference about restaurant operations outside this participant pool.",
    "Interpreting demographic 'treatment' effects (height, personality, color) as real \u2014 they are better read as diagnostics of residual confounding."
  ],
  "quality_issues": [
    "Zero missing values remain across all 52 columns \u2014 because imputation has already been applied. The true pre-imputation missingness is not recoverable from this artifact, so downstream analysis cannot distinguish imputed from observed values.",
    "RESOLVED (was: left-join fan-out). The six one-to-many attribute tables were previously joined raw, inflating 1,161 ratings to 31,559 rows (27.2x) and implicitly weighting each user and restaurant by how many cuisines and payment methods it listed. That biased every descriptive statistic and point estimate, not just the standard errors - mean rating read 0.91 against a true 1.20, and 'informal' dress preference read 68.6% against a true 28.0%. The tables are now collapsed before joining and a guard in merge_data() fails the build if the row count ever stops matching the rating count. Figures below are recomputed on the corrected 1,161-row table.",
    "The Jaccard cuisine-match score was previously degraded to an exact-match indicator: engineer_features() splits each cell on ';', but the un-collapsed merge put a single cuisine in each cell, so the split always returned a one-element set. It now takes 18 distinct values across 123 multi-cuisine users and 158 multi-specialty restaurants.",
    "Most-frequent imputation across 17 categorical columns compresses variance and biases estimates toward the modal category.",
    "RESOLVED. The causal graph derived from this table previously contained 53 cycles; the edge list was re-derived on the corrected table with forward-only layering and is now a strict DAG (14 nodes, 16 edges, 0 cycles). See docs/edge_derivation.md."
  ],
